    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "884\nDOUGLAS DC-3\nYou are flying aboard a Douglas DC-3. It is unlikely we will\nencounter a situation requiring emergency preparations, but it\nis good practice to be acquainted with the safety features pro-\nvided for you on this aircraft.\nEMERGENCY EXITS\nFRONT\nWINDOW EXITS\n1. Remove celuloid\ncover by pulling\nfree.\n2. Turn handle\nclockwise\nshown.\n3. Push window\noutward\nWINDOW\nEXIT\nWINDOW\nEXITS\n4. Grasp sides\nof window-\nleg-body-leg.\n5. Roll over as\nyou go out\nwindow\nDOOR\nEXIT\n6. Sit and slide\noff the wing\nREAR\nMAIN CABIN ENTRANCE DOOR\nTo operate door, grasp handle\nand turn clockwise. Push door\noutward.",
      "FLOTATION EQUIPMENT\n(Life Vests)\nLife Vest\nLocation\nFOR FLIGHTS OVER WATER, LIFE VESTS ARE\nLOCATED BENEATH THE SEATS. DONNING IN-\nSTRUCTIONS ARE ON THE VESTS.\nSEAT BELTS\nWhen the seat belt sign if off you may move around in the\ncabin as much as you like. However, we suggest you keep\nyour seat belt loosely fastened at all times while in your\n, seats.\nBRACE POSITION\nIn an emergency situation your\nflight attendant may request\nyou to assume the Bracing\nposition.\nPORTABLE ELECTRONIC DEVICES\nAre you carrying a portable radio or recorder? Federal\nAviation Agency (FAA) regulation prohibits the operation\nof in-flight of certain portable electronic devices since they\ncan interfere with the aircrafts's navigation system. Please\ncheck with the flight attendant before operating your\nradio, dictating machine or recorder.\nPLEASE DO NOT REMOVE THIS CARD\nFROM AIRCRAFT\n2007.01,059"
    ],
